as we continue our special coverage for  mental health awareness month we're  looking at the mental health strains  that many first-generation immigrants  face in the u.s lilia luciano spoke with  thea pham an asian-american immigrant  who went viral on tick-tock after  sharing her emotional story she talked  about the expectations she says she  faced particularly from members of her  own family  i love my parents  but there is no boundaries  because i am just a wallet now it was a  moment of vulnerability i am the  retirement fund for 27 year old theopham  that resonated with millions on tick  tock i feel trapped and betrayed by my  own culture and my own family  pham is a first generation immigrant  from vietnam she came to the united  states when she was 15 after getting a  full scholarship to a boarding school  what was the plan when you started  applying to these scholarships my  parents struggled a lot financially my  whole job in the family was to focus on  education because that is the way we  lift ourselves out of poverty so that's  a lot of the expectation is put on  myself pham went on to get her phd in  neuroscience from ucla she's now a  biotech venture capitalist  i remember my mom told me like you have  to get a full ride to a college like if  not like don't even bother going home  because like if you return home that's a  failure so you don't have a place to  fall back on at home yeah i feel bad  because i know they love me deeply but  it's also  i felt like a conversation about mental  health and mental wellness is never ever  discussed femme says after posting her  tick tock video she quickly realized  there were other first generation  immigrants who felt the same way do you  remember what your intention was when  you made the video it was just like  literally a video diary of two myself i  had like 20 followers then i was nobody  and i post this video at my most  vulnerable moment  and then all of a sudden i woke up to  like millions of people and i was  freaking out what did it feel like in  that morning after i felt so much shame  because  i love my parents so much and i felt  like i was bringing dishonored by  sharing my struggle and i read all the  comments and i realized like oh my gosh  like i was not alone  hundreds of thousands of people  commented on her video with their own  struggles which left fam wanting to  learn more about their experiences  i'd love to meet you she created  multiple zoom town hall meetings where  first generation immigrants like her  could share their stories together  may champion was a participant i felt it  deep like i felt her tears champion  immigrated from hong kong when she was  five i think we all just feel better at  them the day hearing that you know we're  not the only ones we're not alone  vanessa peso is a licensed trauma  therapist  it is a very brave step to be so  vulnerable and to be so open because in  a lot of different families i think  across the board that's not necessarily  something that's encouraged but one  thing that it does is it almost gives  other people permission  to start to speak up about their own  experiences  pham says she's still working things  through with her parents  but her validation in life no longer  comes from fulfilling  expectations what is success do you know  success is no longer surviving  it's not  it's thriving like i want to thrive  being myself  and have a fulfilling life  i think taking care of yourself and  leading a life that you envision you to  live  would be so much more fulfilling for cbs  mornings lilia luciano in los angeles  we did reach out to fam's parents they  declined to answer any of our questions  so many people suffer in silence it's  just it they feel trapped between these  two cultures and being new americans it  must be really  really troublesome for them i i can new  generation confronting an age-old  problem that a lot of immigrants have  confronted before yeah now she's giving  voice to so many
 

